NICOLAS CAGE VS. NIGHT WOLF HAWK

A battle of wits disguised as an interview

The room was too symmetrical to be safe.

Black walls. Two leather chairs. One table between them. A single lamp hanging overhead like it had a law degree. On the table: two glasses of water, one chessboard, and a small silver card that simply read:

SHADOW FORCE MEDIA UNIT
CONVERSATION IS A WEAPON

Nicolas Cage entered first.

Long coat. Measured steps. The energy of a man who had agreed to an interview and discovered, halfway there, that he had actually consented to ritual combat.

He sat down.

The empty chair across from him remained empty for exactly nine seconds.

Then a side door opened.

In walked Night Wolf Hawk.

Beret. Tiny glasses. Expression unreadable. Posture so controlled it felt pre-edited.

He sat.

Neither man spoke.

A clock ticked.

Then Cage smiled very faintly.

“Interesting.”

Night Wolf Hawk folded his hands.

“You’re earlier than I expected.”

Cage nodded.

“You expected me exactly when I would become useful.”

Night Wolf Hawk’s left eyebrow moved perhaps half a millimeter.

“Useful is an optimistic word.”

Jessica was behind the glass in the observation booth with Little Nick and Micro Nick.

Jessica whispered, “Why does this feel like a summit between two countries that both have black sites?”

Little Nick adjusted binoculars.

“Because it is.”

Micro Nick, standing on the monitor:

“Shh. Opening moves.”

⸻

Night Wolf Hawk leaned forward.

“So. Nicolas Cage.”

Cage leaned forward the exact same amount.

“So. Night Wolf Hawk.”

The lamp above them hummed like it had heard this before.

Night Wolf Hawk spoke first.

“You are difficult to verify.”

Cage smiled.

“I’ve heard the same about your bowel movements.”

Jessica put a hand over her mouth.

Little Nick nearly fell out of his chair.

Night Wolf Hawk did not blink.

“IBS is not a weakness. It is a filtration system.”

Cage nodded solemnly.

“Of course.”

“You mock what you don’t understand.”

“No,” said Cage. “I respect any man whose body has turned on him and yet who still wears a beret indoors.”

Night Wolf Hawk looked down at the chessboard.

Without asking, he moved a pawn.

Cage immediately moved a knight.

Night Wolf Hawk: “Aggressive.”

Cage: “Intuitive.”

Night Wolf Hawk: “Reckless.”

Cage: “Alive.”

Night Wolf Hawk looked up.

“There it is.”

“What?”

“The thing. The performance people mistake for a personality.”

Cage went still.

Not offended.

Interested.

He touched one piece on the board, then withdrew his hand.

“You think I’m performing.”

“I think,” said Night Wolf Hawk, “that you have spent so long being observed that you now observe yourself in real time.”

Cage smiled wider.

“A fair strike.”

Night Wolf Hawk continued.

“You turn sincerity into spectacle so no one can tell where one ends and the other begins.”

Jessica whispered, “Okay, that one actually kind of went hard.”

Little Nick: “He’s probing the perimeter.”

Micro Nick: “No. He’s in the house.”

Cage tapped the rim of his water glass.

“And you,” he said, “turn spectacle into detachment.”

Night Wolf Hawk said nothing.

Cage continued.

“You wear irony like body armor. You speak as if every sentence has already survived a leak. You present yourself as a joke delivered with military posture so that no one gets close enough to ask if there’s a man under it.”

Silence.

Night Wolf Hawk reached for his water.

Didn’t drink it.

“That’s a common misread.”

“No,” said Cage softly. “It’s an uncommon one.”

Night Wolf Hawk’s lips almost twitched.

From the booth, Jessica whispered, “Oh, he felt that.”

Little Nick: “Direct hit.”

Micro Nick: “Thoracic cavity.”

⸻

Night Wolf Hawk crossed one leg over the other.

“Let’s proceed.”

Cage nodded.

“Please.”

Night Wolf Hawk opened a folder.

It was empty.

He looked at it anyway.

“Question one. Are you a serious actor trapped in a meme, or a meme trapped in a serious actor?”

Cage considered.

Then: “Neither. I am a weather system.”

Night Wolf Hawk nodded like that answer had been filed years ago.

“Expected.”

Cage tilted his head.

“And you? Are you a comedian pretending to be a covert operative, or a covert operative pretending to be a comedian?”

Night Wolf Hawk’s face did not move.

“Yes.”

Little Nick slapped the table in the booth.

“Oh, he’s good.”

Jessica: “That was infuriatingly clean.”

Cage chuckled.

“Fair.”

Night Wolf Hawk turned a page in the empty folder.

“Question two. What is the first thing you do when you realize everyone in the room thinks you’re crazy?”

Cage answered instantly.

“Commit.”

Night Wolf Hawk: “Why?”

“Because hesitation is confession.”

Night Wolf Hawk gave a tiny nod.

Then Cage asked, “Your turn. What do you do when everyone in the room underestimates you?”

Night Wolf Hawk: “Let them finish.”

Cage: “And then?”

Night Wolf Hawk looked directly at him.

“Invoice them.”

Jessica wheezed.

Micro Nick scribbled something in a tiny notebook.

Little Nick read over his shoulder.

“What’s that?”

Micro Nick: “Cold lines.”

⸻

The chessboard was getting ugly now.

Neither man played for beauty. They played for nerve.

Night Wolf Hawk sacrificed a bishop.

Cage did not react.

Night Wolf Hawk watched him very carefully.

“Do you know why people watch you?”

Cage leaned back.

“Because something in me gives them permission to stop pretending they’re not strange.”

The room changed temperature.

Even Night Wolf Hawk took a beat at that one.

Then he said, “Not bad.”

Cage nodded. “Your turn.”

Night Wolf Hawk steepled his fingers.

“People watch me because I say absurd things with the body language of accountability.”

Cage laughed once. Sharp. Delighted.

“Yes.”

Night Wolf Hawk continued, “If you say madness with enough confidence, people stop asking whether it’s true and start wondering whether they’re late.”

Cage’s eyes lit up.

“My God.”

Night Wolf Hawk: “What?”

Cage: “You’re not a comedian.”

Night Wolf Hawk went very still.

Jessica leaned into the glass.

Little Nick whispered, “Here we go.”

Cage leaned across the table like he was revealing the location of buried gold.

“You’re a bureaucrat of unreality.”

Night Wolf Hawk stared at him.

Cage warmed to it.

“You file nonsense in triplicate. You deliver delusion with procedural certainty. You don’t tell jokes—you issue memos from a dimension with poor oversight.”

For the first time all night, Night Wolf Hawk actually smiled.

It was small.

Dangerous.

Correct.

“Now we’re interviewing.”

