The lamp buzzed louder.

Somewhere in the wall, machinery clicked.

Night Wolf Hawk glanced at the ceiling once.

Then back to Cage.

“You’ve noticed, of course, that this is not a normal interview.”

Cage gave him a look.

“Night Wolf Hawk, I have never had a normal interview.”

“Good.”

Night Wolf Hawk slid the silver card across the table.

Cage looked at it.

Turned it over.

On the back:

ONLY ONE OF YOU LEAVES WITH THE TRUTH

Cage looked up.

Night Wolf Hawk was already standing.

Jessica: “Oh, come on.”

Little Nick: “Classic escalation.”

Micro Nick: “Page two.”

Cage rose slowly.

“Are we enemies now?”

Night Wolf Hawk adjusted his cuffs.

“No.”

“Rivals?”

“No.”

Cage smiled.

“What, then?”

Night Wolf Hawk moved toward the side door.

At the threshold, he stopped.

Turned slightly.

And said:

“Parallel jurisdictions.”

Then he left.

The room went dark.

The chessboard lit up from beneath.

Every piece on it had become a tiny Nicolas Cage.

Jessica: “Absolutely not.”

Little Nick: “He booby-trapped the metaphor.”

Micro Nick pointed to the board.

“Checkmate.”

Cage looked down.

Black king cornered. White queen in place.

One final note etched into the wood:

YOU WERE NEVER PLAYING CHESS.
YOU WERE PLAYING AUDIENCE CAPTURE.

Cage stared at it.

Then, very slowly, he laughed.

Not because he’d lost.

Because he’d been seen.

And from somewhere in the ventilation system, Night Wolf Hawk’s voice drifted down one last time:

“I never scream, Nicolas.”

Cage looked up at the darkness and said:

“You don’t have to.”

